/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> setFillSixteenthSinglesLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "At the pad (\"Sixteenth Singles Fill\") you learned to leave the groove and land on the one. On the kit the same singles travel over snare and toms, the kick keeps 1 and 3 going underneath, and the one after the fill is a crash with the kick: exactly what the band hears.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Three bars of rock eighths: hi-hat eighths, snare on 2 and 4, kick on 1 and 3; bar 1 starts with the crash instead of the hi-hat. In the fill bar both hands leave the hat. Line 1 plays two beats on the snare, beat 3 on the high tom, beat 4 on the floor tom, and the right hand starts every drum. The kick does not stop: 1 and 3, under the fill too.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Line 2 walks down one drum per beat (snare, high tom, mid tom, floor tom) and doubles from eighths to sixteenths halfway. Line 4 puts the accents in threes across high tom and floor tom; line 7 visits all four toms and leaves holes. Line 8 plays the thirty-second burst on the floor tom and comes back to the snare for beat 4. If the crash is late, play the fill on the snare alone first, like the pad sheet, then add the toms. Toms accept any tom and the crash any crash, so a kit with fewer drums plays the sheet too: the mid tom and the second floor tom go to the nearest tom you have.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "The tom run before the chorus in nearly every rock and pop song; Phil Collins' famous break in \"In the Air Tonight\" is sixteenth notes down the toms.",
  ),
];
